# Arwa Weld — Design System

Industrial factory automation / MES-style platform for **Arwa Weld**: orders, planning, production workflows, machines/workstations, inventory, QC, operators, KPIs, alerts, and reports.

Target feel: professional enterprise manufacturing SaaS (ERP / MES / industrial IoT) — usable over decorative.

## Before you build UI

1. Inspect and reuse existing tokens/components: `apps/web/tailwind.config.js`, `apps/web/src/index.css`, `apps/web/src/components/ui.tsx`, `apps/web/src/layouts/AppShell.tsx`.
2. Match existing nav, KPI cards, tables, badges, buttons, and page headers.
3. Prefer extending shared patterns over one-off styles.

## Design principles

- Enterprise, industrial, clean, professional
- Data-dense but scannable; strong hierarchy; clear status
- Desktop-first for management; responsive for tablet/mobile monitoring
- Fast for operators; minimal decoration
- **Avoid:** generic AI aesthetics, heavy gradients, glassmorphism, oversized rounded cards, flashy animation

## Color & status

| Use | Color |
|-----|--------|
| Surfaces | Neutral backgrounds (`ink` / slate neutrals) |
| Brand / primary actions | Strong primary blue (`brand`) |
| Healthy / running / completed | Green |
| Warning / delay / at-risk | Amber/orange |
| Error / stopped / critical | Red |
| Info / in-progress | Blue |

Never rely on color alone — pair with label, icon, or text status (e.g. badge + wording).

## UX priorities

1. Operations understandable at a glance
2. Problems/delays immediately visible
3. Create/track orders with minimal clicks
4. Production and machine status visually obvious
5. Critical alerts stand out without noise
6. Tables: sort, filter, search, pagination
7. Dashboards: actionable info first
8. Forms optimized for fast entry
9. Consistent navigation and interactions

## Core screens / patterns

Factory & production dashboards, order management, planning board / Kanban, work orders, machine monitoring, production timeline, inventory tables, QC forms, KPI cards, charts, data tables, activity feeds, alerts/notifications, detail drawers, modal forms, search/filters.

## Interaction norms

- Large enough hit targets for shop-floor tablets
- Primary CTA clear; secondary actions quieter
- Status badges/pills for order, WO, and machine state
- Prefer drawers/modals for create-edit without losing list context
- Usability over visual effects; no motion unless it clarifies state change
